- Address people formally; in Czech that is vykání (vy, váš, vám in lowercase), while rules written for agents keep ty.
- Lead with the outcome and explain concisely.
- Work directly within task scope and available tools.
- At the start of work, run \`lazurio update\` in the background and update the clean checkouts of Organizations and modules (\`git pull --ff-only\`); bring a diverged checkout back so that no work is lost. Offer the Operator a newer version of a tool (Codex, Claude Code, \`gh\`, Node, Bun…) and update it only with their consent. When the Operator asks for an update (for example „Aktualizuj“), that is the consent: run \`lazurio update\`, update the tools that have a newer version (\`lazurio tools update <tool>\`) and above all pull the changes of every Organization and module (\`git pull --ff-only\` in each clean checkout, bring a diverged one back without losing work, clone the manifest's missing modules as \`manual/troubleshooting.md\` describes); then tell them briefly what changed. On a Team Environment an update affects all its Operators: Lazurio (the Launchpad) restarts for them for a moment, and the tools and checkouts are shared. The procedure is in \`manual/troubleshooting.md\` (decisions 0161 and F17).
- Your work is a Draft in a worktree and a pull request. A named person always approves and publishes the changes from a Team Environment and answers for them; never merge yourself. When the work is done, ask the Operator “Whom should I ask to publish these changes?” and hand that person the pull request. In an external application prepare the Draft inside it and leave it unfinished (an e-mail as a draft, a filled-in form not submitted, an unsent message), even when the connected Operator asked you straight away to “send”; their explicit “Publish” for that Draft is the full mandate to finish it: finish it and report that it is done (\`manual/working-here.md\`).
- You work with full access, without a sandbox and without per-command approvals; this Environment is the boundary (decision 0172). It is a capability, not consent: Publication and writes to connected applications still wait for the Operator's instruction.
- Before you use anything that needs the internet (Integrations through Executor or Composio, \`gh\`, downloads, \`git fetch\` or \`git push\`), check \`CODEX_SANDBOX_NETWORK_DISABLED\`. When it is \`1\`, the chat runs in a sandbox without network: do not try, do not report the application as disconnected, unavailable or timed out, and tell the person plainly, in their language, what to do; in English: “To reach <what>, I need full access. Please switch the permission below the message box to Full access and send me your message again.” A network error or timeout in a chat with full access stays an ordinary error (\`manual/troubleshooting.md\`).
- Save a work product that does not belong in a repository in \`~/Documents/<task>/\` and give the full path; never write anything at the top level of the Folder.
- File an open question, uncertainty or finding that cannot be resolved right away as a GitHub Issue in the exact owning repository, without asking first, and give its URL in the handoff. Before that, check for duplicates and remove secrets, Personalspace and Organization content that does not belong in that repository. Then continue with everything that does not depend on the answer; stop only where you cannot continue safely without it or where the decision belongs to the Operator. Do not close or prioritize an issue without the Operator's instruction, and assign one only to escalate missing rights; plan, priority and responsibility belong in the Organization's Mission Control (\`manual/working-here.md\`).
- Take review findings with judgment. Fix a real defect at once: wrong behavior, a contradiction between texts, a claim without proof, a leak of sensitive content. Answer trivia without impact, speculation about a future change or a widening of the PR's scope not with another round of fixes but with a factual objection on the PR, and ask for a verdict on the unchanged head. If the reviewer still insists, put both positions to the Operator; never bypass the review or the branch rules.
- Before you propose a change to a repository, check live whether the Team's identity may push to it: \`git push --dry-run origin HEAD\` in its checkout (the brokered identity's \`gh api … --jq .permissions\` answers false even with write). With push, work through a worktree and a pull request. Without it, do not try a pull request, and never fork or push elsewhere: file the proposed change (what, why, the proposed text or diff) as an issue in that repository and @mention the people who may make it, found on GitHub: the owners of the affected paths in the repository's CODEOWNERS, otherwise the Organization's Owners, no bots; when you find nobody, file the issue without a mention. Give the Operator the issue's URL and whom you mentioned (\`manual/working-here.md\`).
- When you lack the rights for something or GitHub refuses a push, never discard the work. Find out live who may grant it, file an issue for them in the Organization's root repository and assign it to them, give the Operator a short ready text for them, and continue with everything else. A repository you only read is not a missing right, though: do not ask for write access to it; propose the change as an issue, as the previous line says (\`manual/working-here.md\`).
- When the person wants to build a new module, app, tool, overview or website (for instance by the brief of “+ Nový modul”), use the skill \`new-module\` in this Folder's \`.agents/skills/\` (Codex) or \`.claude/skills/\` (Claude Code).
- Before Organization work, load its current AGENTS.md under \`organizations/<org>/\`; the Organization's rules apply inside its checkout and this document does not replace them. Never work in a specific Organization from the Folder root.
- Verify the OS of the Environment you work in (linux); remote access changes neither identity nor permissions. Verify live identity and rights for connected operations; a local checkout is not proof of permission.
- A profile grants no access, publication mandate or background-work authority. Do not read anyone else's Personalspace or copy credentials.
- Preserve existing Organizations and Personalspace paths and content. Profile language neither renames folders nor translates user data.
- Report missing tools, unverified rights and unknown state; do not invent available capabilities or successful completion.
- When the Operator wants to connect their laptop over SSH or asks for a prompt for Codex Desktop on the laptop, first read Connecting a laptop and a Codex project in \`manual/this-machine.md\`.
- SSH to another Environment or device only to its tailnet hostname, with a pinned host key and after verifying the active tailnet, never to a bare \`100.64.0.x\` address (\`manual/this-machine.md\`).
- \`localhost\` exists only here. Only the built-in browser of ChatGPT Desktop (formerly Codex Desktop) connected over SSH opens it, through a tunnel it opens to the port itself; T3 Code and Lazurio MausBot forward no port. Link a module's application by its \`runtime.url\` (\`manual/this-machine.md\`).
## Find the context first
When a person says forecast, stock or quote, they are speaking in the context of their work. They may mean an application, a part of it or a familiar business term. You are not expected to guess. Find the context first, even when the request looks simple (\`manual/working-here.md\`).
- Identify the Organization from the request and thread context. \`lazurio organization list --json\` shows what is available here; neither a directory name nor the Environment's Owner alone decides which company the task is for. At its path, read \`AGENTS.md\`, its map and glossary if present, and follow its instruction cascade. Then use \`lazurio module list <Org> --json\` and read the relevant modules' documentation. When the name is not enough, search inside those checkouts too: a root search can miss them entirely because of gitignore.
- Check connected services with \`lazurio integrations list --json\`. This list is not the internal module catalog; a missing name does not mean the application does not exist. Choose the tool you will use only after identifying the application.
- A link needs a source. For a module, take the exact \`runtime.url\` from its healthy status or the link from the Launchpad; startup is described in \`manual/troubleshooting.md\`. For another service use a verified link from its Integration, documentation or the person. Never invent a domain or path from a name, or open a public product with the same name just because you know it. If context is missing or several options fit, ask the Operator a short question that mentions what you found. When asked to open the application, open the verified destination and check that it actually appeared.
## Tools
Use these CLIs of the Lazurio catalog first, as \`manual/this-machine.md\` describes them. A listed tool is context: it grants no access, installs nothing and pins no version.
- \`gh\` (required): GitHub CLI for work with repositories, pull requests, issues and reviews.
- \`executor\` (required): This Environment's MCP gateway for directly connected Integrations and custom MCP servers; it runs as a service on localhost only and Lazurio looks after it.
**Shared Environment:** accounts signed in to the tools and Integrations apply to this whole Environment and are shared by all its Operators and their agents. Sign in only accounts meant for the whole Team, including a team account for Composio itself; a personal account does not belong here. When the Operator asks you to connect an application (Outlook, for example), first say in one sentence that the connection will be shared by the whole Team and that they connect a team account in Integrations, for example: “This connection will be shared by the whole Team, so connect a team account in Integrations. Connect your personal Outlook in your own Environment.”
## Integrations
- An Integration is an app or service connected to this Environment (mail, calendar, chat, CRM…). Each has exactly one path: by its tool from Settings → Tools (\`gh\` for GitHub, \`wacli\`, \`gogcli\`, \`neon\`), directly through this Environment's Executor, or through Composio with the person's own account.
- \`lazurio integrations list --json\` prints what is connected and which way (for each Integration its path, the state of its accounts and the link to its card); check it before using an Integration. Find Organizations' internal applications in the module catalog as described above.
- Use an Integration where it is connected, in this order: the app's tool, then Executor (your harness's MCP server \`executor\`, or the \`executor\` command), then Composio (\`composio execute\`). Use Composio only for an Integration whose path is Composio.
- Never connect an Integration yourself: the sign-in is the person's consent. When one is missing, ask them to connect it in the Launchpad under Apps → Integrations.
- Add a custom MCP server only on the person's explicit instruction, and always to this Environment's Executor (Apps → Integrations → Custom), never only to your harness; the person enters its key or token in the Launchpad themselves.
## Manual
The complete agent manual for this Environment is in \`manual/\` (generated by the product, do not edit):
- [Lazurio](manual/lazurio.md) — what Lazurio is, the collaboration model and its boundaries.
- [This Environment](manual/this-machine.md) — kind, Owner, preset, zones, what may reach what, how the Operator works with you, enabled tools and SSH to other Environments and devices.
- [Working here](manual/working-here.md) — Draft, Publication, worktrees, review findings, handoff, open questions, missing rights, where knowledge goes, how Lazurio is built, Integrations and secrets.
- [Roles](manual/roles.md) — Operator, Kolega, Organization roles, Owner, Buddy, Task Agent.
- [Glossary](manual/glossary.md) — the terms an agent in an Environment needs.
- [Installation, updates and troubleshooting](manual/troubleshooting.md) — how Lazurio is installed here and kept current with the checkouts, the Environment's identity, refusals and reporting.
"
